    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.3 Stakeholders | Foundational', "question": 'The role that authorizes the initiative, provides financial resources and approves scope is:', "options": ['A) Domain SME', 'B) Sponsor', 'C) End User', 'D) Tester'], "answer": 'B) Sponsor', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.3: The Sponsor authorizes the initiative, provides resources and approves key scope decisions.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.3 Stakeholders | Foundational', "question": 'A QA team that validates the solution meets the requirements BEST represents:', "options": ['A) Tester', 'B) Operational Support', 'C) Supplier', 'D) Domain SME'], "answer": 'A) Tester', "explanation": "BABOK® v3 §2.3: Those who validate the solution's quality against the requirements are Tester stakeholders."},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.3 Stakeholders | Foundational', "question": 'The IT operations team responsible for post-go-live maintenance BEST represents:', "options": ['A) End User', 'B) Tester', 'C) Operational Support', 'D) Sponsor'], "answer": 'C) Operational Support', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.3: Those responsible for support and maintenance after deployment are Operational Support.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.3 Stakeholders | Foundational', "question": 'An external vendor whose product will integrate with the solution BEST represents:', "options": ['A) End User', 'B) Supplier', 'C) Sponsor', 'D) Tester'], "answer": 'B) Supplier', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.3: A vendor that provides integrated products or services is a Supplier stakeholder.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.3 Stakeholders | Foundational', "question": 'An expert in accounting regulation who provides specialized knowledge BEST represents:', "options": ['A) Sponsor', 'B) Supplier', 'C) Domain SME', 'D) Customer'], "answer": 'C) Domain SME', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.3: Domain SMEs provide specialized knowledge of a business area.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.3 Stakeholders | Foundational', "question": "A regulator who doesn't use the system but approves its outputs — is this a stakeholder per BABOK® v3?", "options": ['A) No, only direct users are', 'B) Yes, they have a relationship with the change and its outputs', 'C) No, external parties never are', 'D) Only if they attend meetings'], "answer": 'B) Yes, they have a relationship with the change and its outputs', "explanation": "BABOK® v3 §2.3: Regulators are stakeholders by virtue of their relationship to the outcomes of the change, even if they don't use the system."},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.3 Stakeholders | Intermediate', "question": 'A HIGH-influence, LOW-interest stakeholder is BEST managed by:', "options": ['A) By removing them from the register', 'B) By ignoring them', 'C) By involving them intensively in everything', 'D) By keeping them informed at key milestones and consulting them when their authority is needed'], "answer": 'D) By keeping them informed at key milestones and consulting them when their authority is needed', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.3: High-influence/low-interest stakeholders are kept satisfied and informed without overwhelming them with detail.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.3 Stakeholders | Intermediate', "question": 'A LOW-influence, HIGH-interest stakeholder with deep domain knowledge is BEST managed by:', "options": ['A) Only at project closure', 'B) By ignoring them for lack of authority', 'C) By keeping them informed and leveraging their knowledge and potential support', 'D) By naming them co-sponsor'], "answer": 'C) By keeping them informed and leveraging their knowledge and potential support', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.3: High-interest/low-influence stakeholders often have deep domain knowledge and can be strong allies.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.3 Stakeholders | Intermediate', "question": 'A BA omits the legal department during identification; compliance problems then arise, with rework. This BEST illustrates:', "options": ['A) Insufficient testing', 'B) Incomplete stakeholder identification leading to missed requirements and late rework', 'C) Weak governance', 'D) Poor documentation'], "answer": 'B) Incomplete stakeholder identification leading to missed requirements and late rework', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.3: Omitting stakeholders leads to missed requirements discovered late, when they are most costly to resolve.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.3 Stakeholders | Intermediate', "question": 'Per BABOK® v3, the business analyst themselves:', "options": ['A) Manages all other stakeholders', 'B) Should not document their own interests', 'C) Takes priority over the others', 'D) Is also a stakeholder with a relationship to the change and its outcomes'], "answer": 'D) Is also a stakeholder with a relationship to the change and its outcomes', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.3: BABOK® explicitly recognizes the BA as a stakeholder with a relationship to the change.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.3 Stakeholders | Advanced', "question": 'Two stakeholder groups have directly conflicting needs. The MOST appropriate action is:', "options": ['A) Document both and let development decide', 'B) Facilitate resolution by understanding both perspectives, evaluating business value, and escalating to the sponsor if there is no consensus', 'C) Remove one group from the register', 'D) Automatically prioritize the most senior one'], "answer": 'B) Facilitate resolution by understanding both perspectives, evaluating business value, and escalating to the sponsor if there is no consensus', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.3: Conflicting needs require facilitated resolution with objective value assessment and clear escalation.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.3 Stakeholders | Advanced', "question": 'Mid-project, it is discovered that the unidentified IT security team has requirements that impact the solution. The MOST appropriate response is:', "options": ['A) Wait until the end of development', 'B) Proceed without engaging them to avoid delay', 'C) Add them to the register, analyze their requirements and influence, and plan their engagement immediately', "D) Leave it in the PM's hands"], "answer": 'C) Add them to the register, analyze their requirements and influence, and plan their engagement immediately', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.3: Discovering a missing stakeholder demands immediate action before rework occurs.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.3 Stakeholders | Expert', "question": 'A BA manages 85 stakeholder groups across 14 countries. The program director says engaging everyone is impractical. The resolution MOST aligned with BABOK® v3 is:', "options": ['A) Delegate everything to local PMs without oversight', 'B) Cut down to the 15 with the largest budget', 'C) Only those in the first wave', 'D) Group by shared characteristics and design representative engagement, ensuring the critical groups in each cluster are engaged directly'], "answer": 'D) Group by shared characteristics and design representative engagement, ensuring the critical groups in each cluster are engaged directly', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.3: With large numbers, grouping by characteristics with representative engagement is the BABOK®-aligned approach — not exclusion.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.4 Requirements vs Designs | Foundational', "question": 'Per BABOK® v3, the BEST distinction between requirements and designs is:', "options": ['A) Requirements are high-level; designs are always detailed', 'B) Requirements are for the business; designs are only for IT', 'C) Requirements are written by the BA; designs by the architect', 'D) Requirements describe what is needed; designs describe how the solution will satisfy it'], "answer": 'D) Requirements describe what is needed; designs describe how the solution will satisfy it', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.4: Requirements define the what/why; designs define the how, preserving solution flexibility.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.4 Requirements vs Designs | Foundational', "question": 'A database schema with tables, columns and relationships is BEST classified as:', "options": ['A) Transition requirement', 'B) Functional requirement', 'C) Design artifact', 'D) Non-functional requirement'], "answer": 'C) Design artifact', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.4: A database schema specifies how the data will be structured — it is a design artifact.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.4 Requirements vs Designs | Foundational', "question": "'Users must be able to search products by name, category and price range.' This is BEST classified as:", "options": ['A) Non-functional requirement', 'B) Business requirement', 'C) Functional solution requirement', 'D) Design artifact'], "answer": 'C) Functional solution requirement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.4: Multi-criteria search is a concrete system capability — a Functional solution requirement.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.4 Requirements vs Designs | Foundational', "question": 'A wireframe showing the layout and navigation of a mobile screen is BEST classified as:', "options": ['A) Stakeholder requirement', 'B) Design artifact', 'C) Functional requirement', 'D) Business requirement'], "answer": 'B) Design artifact', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.4: A wireframe describes how the solution will look and behave — it is a design artifact.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.4 Requirements vs Designs | Intermediate', "question": "A BA documents both 'what the system must do' and 'how the UI must look' in the same artifact. This MOST risks:", "options": ['A) Violating BABOK® standards', 'B) Blurring the requirement/design boundary and unnecessarily constraining solution options', 'C) Nothing, it improves efficiency', 'D) Confusing stakeholders about the technology'], "answer": 'B) Blurring the requirement/design boundary and unnecessarily constraining solution options', "explanation": "BABOK® v3 §2.4: Mixing requirements and designs risks premature commitment and reduces the design team's flexibility."},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.4 Requirements vs Designs | Intermediate', "question": "'The system must calculate loan payments using the French amortization method.' Per BABOK® v3, this is BEST:", "options": ['A) A non-functional requirement', 'B) A functional solution requirement: it defines what must be calculated without imposing the code implementation', 'C) A design constraint because it specifies an algorithm', 'D) A business requirement'], "answer": 'B) A functional solution requirement: it defines what must be calculated without imposing the code implementation', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.4: Specifying the calculation method defines the behavior (what it does), not the internal implementation; it remains functional.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.4 Requirements vs Designs | Advanced', "question": 'A development team treats a future-state process model (BPMN) as a design specification. The BA argues it is both. Which BABOK® v3 principle supports this?', "options": ['A) Only textual artifacts are requirements', 'B) Designs are always created after all requirements are finalized', 'C) The same artifact can serve as a requirement and as a design depending on the level of abstraction and the audience', 'D) Requirements and designs are always mutually exclusive'], "answer": 'C) The same artifact can serve as a requirement and as a design depending on the level of abstraction and the audience', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.4: Artifacts can serve a dual purpose depending on the level of abstraction and the audience consuming them.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.4 Requirements vs Designs | Advanced', "question": 'A BA argues that requirements and design should go in separate documents. The MAIN reason is:', "options": ['A) Keeping them separate allows the design to be revised or replaced without altering the requirements, preserving the integrity of the need independently of the solution', 'B) BABOK® mandates it', 'C) It duplicates documentation effort', 'D) Satisfy audit'], "answer": 'A) Keeping them separate allows the design to be revised or replaced without altering the requirements, preserving the integrity of the need independently of the solution', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.4: Separation allows multiple design iterations without corrupting the requirements baseline.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.4 Requirements vs Designs | Expert', "question": 'Two analysts debate whether a swimlane diagram is a requirement or a design. The MOST precise answer per BABOK® v3 is:', "options": ['A) It depends on abstraction and intent: if it describes what the process must achieve without dictating implementation, it leans toward a requirement; if it specifies how the system will orchestrate the steps, it leans toward a design', 'B) It is irrelevant as long as stakeholders approve it', 'C) It is always design: diagrams are design tools', 'D) It is always a requirement'], "answer": 'A) It depends on abstraction and intent: if it describes what the process must achieve without dictating implementation, it leans toward a requirement; if it specifies how the system will orchestrate the steps, it leans toward a design', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.4: The classification depends on the level of abstraction and intent — the same type of diagram can be a requirement or a design.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.5 Integration | Foundational', "question": 'When starting a new initiative, which activity must come BEFORE requirements elicitation?', "options": ['A) Identify stakeholders and understand the Need and the Context', 'B) Write functional requirements', 'C) Design the architecture', 'D) Develop test cases'], "answer": 'A) Identify stakeholders and understand the Need and the Context', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.1, §2.3: Understanding who the stakeholders are and which Need to address — in which Context — precedes elicitation.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.5 Integration | Intermediate', "question": "A BA reviews a requirements document and asks: 'If these are met, do they actually solve the original business problem?' This BEST describes:", "options": ['A) Requirements validation', 'B) Requirements baseline', 'C) Requirements verification', 'D) Prioritization'], "answer": 'A) Requirements validation', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: Validation checks that the requirements will lead to a solution that addresses the real Need and delivers Value.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.5 Integration | Intermediate', "question": 'A context diagram showing the system boundary, external entities and data flows relates MOST to defining which BACCM concept?', "options": ['A) Need', 'B) Context', 'C) Value', 'D) Stakeholder'], "answer": 'B) Context', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.1: A context diagram defines the solution boundary and its environment — it defines the Context.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.5 Integration | Advanced', "question": "A BA is told: 'We already know the solution, just document its requirements.' The response MOST aligned with BABOK® v3 is:", "options": ['A) Ask the PM to resolve it', 'B) Accept and document requirements for the pre-selected solution', 'C) Refuse to work', 'D) Challenge the solution by first examining the BACCM Need, ensuring it addresses the real problem before investing in documentation'], "answer": 'D) Challenge the solution by first examining the BACCM Need, ensuring it addresses the real problem before investing in documentation', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.1: A pre-selected solution is a classic anti-pattern; the BA must validate Solution–Need alignment first.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.5 Integration | Expert', "question": 'A program delivers all approved requirements on time and on budget. Six months later, benefit realization is only 40%. The MOST complete explanation per BABOK® v3 is:', "options": ['A) The PM should have escalated earlier', 'B) Development team errors', 'C) The program was too large', 'D) The requirements were approved but not validated against the real Need and Value, or the Context changed after delivery and no Solution Evaluation was done'], "answer": 'D) The requirements were approved but not validated against the real Need and Value, or the Context changed after delivery and no Solution Evaluation was done', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: Approved requirements do not guarantee value; validation against Need and Value and post-delivery monitoring are essential.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.1 BACCM | Foundational', "question": 'Which of these is NOT a BACCM concept?', "options": ['A) Value', 'B) Requirement', 'C) Stakeholder', 'D) Context'], "answer": 'B) Requirement', "explanation": "BABOK® v3 §2.1: 'Requirement' is not one of the six BACCM concepts (Change, Need, Solution, Stakeholder, Value, Context)."},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.1 BACCM | Foundational', "question": 'The BACCM concept MOST directly related to why a change is undertaken is:', "options": ['A) Context', 'B) Need', 'C) Solution', 'D) Value'], "answer": 'B) Need', "explanation": "BABOK® v3 §2.1: The Need captures the problem or opportunity that motivates the change — the 'why'."},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.1 BACCM | Foundational', "question": 'A company moves from paper reports to a digital system. The transition itself represents:', "options": ['A) Change', 'B) Need', 'C) Solution', 'D) Context'], "answer": 'A) Change', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.1: The act of moving from one state to another is Change.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.1 BACCM | Foundational', "question": 'Operating in a heavily regulated healthcare industry represents, in the BACCM:', "options": ['A) Stakeholder', 'B) Context', 'C) Value', 'D) Need'], "answer": 'B) Context', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.1: The industry and the regulatory environment are part of the Context.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.1 BACCM | Foundational', "question": 'The BACCM contains exactly:', "options": ['A) Four concepts', 'B) Five concepts', 'C) Six concepts', 'D) Seven concepts'], "answer": 'C) Six concepts', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.1: The BACCM contains six core concepts.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.1 BACCM | Intermediate', "question": "A facilitator redirects a workshop from 'what features do we want' toward 'what problem are we solving'. They prioritize the concept:", "options": ['A) Solution', 'B) Value', 'C) Change', 'D) Need'], "answer": 'D) Need', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.1: Redirecting from desired features to the underlying problem is prioritizing the Need.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.1 BACCM | Intermediate', "question": 'A BACCM analysis shows a clear Need, Context and Stakeholders, but undefined Value. The MOST significant risk is:', "options": ['A) There will be no agreed basis to measure success or decide trade-offs', "B) Developers won't know what to build", 'C) The sponsor will pull funding', 'D) The project will take too long'], "answer": 'A) There will be no agreed basis to measure success or decide trade-offs', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.1: Without a defined Value there is no success criterion; decisions become subjective.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.1 BACCM | Intermediate', "question": 'A global recession begins during a two-year transformation, invalidating demand assumptions. In the BACCM, what changed?', "options": ['A) The Need only', 'B) The Context, and potentially the Need', 'C) The Stakeholders only', 'D) The Solution'], "answer": 'B) The Context, and potentially the Need', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.1: A macroeconomic change alters the Context; if customer behavior changed, the Need may require re-evaluation.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.1 BACCM | Advanced', "question": 'A BA documents the Context (regulation, competition, culture). The BEST reason per BABOK® v3 is:', "options": ['A) Context only matters in large programs', 'B) It replaces stakeholder analysis', 'C) Satisfy audit', 'D) The Context defines the circumstances that shape and constrain the other concepts; a misunderstood one produces misaligned solutions'], "answer": 'D) The Context defines the circumstances that shape and constrain the other concepts; a misunderstood one produces misaligned solutions', "explanation": "BABOK® v3 §2.1: Context shapes all the other concepts; ignoring it produces solutions for a reality that doesn't exist."},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.1 BACCM | Advanced', "question": 'A BA evaluates a deployed solution and finds that the original Need has changed. Which concepts and knowledge areas are MOST relevant?', "options": ['A) Solution Evaluation only', 'B) Requirements Life Cycle Management only', 'C) Need and Context (BACCM) shifted; Strategy Analysis and Solution Evaluation are both relevant', 'D) The BACCM does not apply post-deployment'], "answer": 'C) Need and Context (BACCM) shifted; Strategy Analysis and Solution Evaluation are both relevant', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.1: When Need and Context change, Solution Evaluation and Strategy Analysis are triggered together.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.1 BACCM | Expert', "question": 'A BA proposes using the BACCM as the intake framework for every new initiative. The MOST complete justification is:', "options": ['A) By examining Need, Context, Stakeholders, Value, Change and Solution at intake, the organization filters out misaligned initiatives before committing resources', 'B) It satisfies IIBA requirements', 'C) It is the fastest intake method', 'D) It replaces the project charter'], "answer": 'A) By examining Need, Context, Stakeholders, Value, Change and Solution at intake, the organization filters out misaligned initiatives before committing resources', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.1: The BACCM as an intake framework prevents the most expensive mistake: funding the wrong initiatives.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Foundational', "question": "'Achieve a 90% customer satisfaction score within 12 months.' This is BEST classified as:", "options": ['A) Business requirement', 'B) Stakeholder requirement', 'C) Functional solution requirement', 'D) Non-functional solution requirement'], "answer": 'A) Business requirement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: A high-level organizational success measure is a Business requirement.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Foundational', "question": "'The application must support English, French and Spanish.' This is BEST classified as:", "options": ['A) Business requirement', 'B) Stakeholder requirement', 'C) Functional solution requirement', 'D) Non-functional solution requirement'], "answer": 'D) Non-functional solution requirement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: Language support is a quality/capability constraint — a Non-functional solution requirement.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Foundational', "question": "'The system must allow submitting purchase requisitions for approval.' This is BEST classified as:", "options": ['A) Business requirement', 'B) Stakeholder requirement', 'C) Functional solution requirement', 'D) Non-functional solution requirement'], "answer": 'C) Functional solution requirement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: Submission-for-approval functionality is a concrete capability — a Functional solution requirement.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Intermediate', "question": "'The system must retain data for a minimum of 7 years per financial regulation.' This is BEST classified as:", "options": ['A) Business requirement', 'B) Stakeholder requirement', 'C) Functional solution requirement', 'D) Non-functional solution requirement'], "answer": 'D) Non-functional solution requirement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: Data retention is a compliance constraint — a Non-functional solution requirement.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Intermediate', "question": "'The system must support concurrent access for up to 5,000 users without degrading response time.' This is BEST classified as:", "options": ['A) Business requirement', 'B) Stakeholder requirement', 'C) Functional solution requirement', 'D) Non-functional solution requirement'], "answer": 'D) Non-functional solution requirement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: Concurrency is a scalability/performance attribute — a Non-functional solution requirement.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Intermediate', "question": "'As sales director, I need a weekly revenue forecast report by region and product line.' This is BEST classified as:", "options": ['A) Business requirement', 'B) Stakeholder requirement', 'C) Functional solution requirement', 'D) Non-functional solution requirement'], "answer": 'B) Stakeholder requirement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: A reporting need from a specific role is a Stakeholder requirement.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Intermediate', "question": "'The system must be available 24/7 globally with no scheduled maintenance windows.' This is BEST classified as:", "options": ['A) Business requirement', 'B) Stakeholder requirement', 'C) Functional solution requirement', 'D) Non-functional solution requirement'], "answer": 'D) Non-functional solution requirement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: Global 24/7 availability is a reliability attribute — a Non-functional solution requirement.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Foundational', "question": "'The system must back up all data daily at 02:00 and retain copies for 90 days.' This is BEST classified as:", "options": ['A) Business requirement', 'B) Stakeholder requirement', 'C) Functional solution requirement', 'D) Non-functional solution requirement'], "answer": 'D) Non-functional solution requirement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: Backup frequency and retention are operational quality attributes — a Non-functional solution requirement.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Intermediate', "question": "'The system must let customers track their orders in real time.' This is BEST classified as:", "options": ['A) Business requirement', 'B) Stakeholder requirement', 'C) Functional solution requirement', 'D) Non-functional solution requirement'], "answer": 'C) Functional solution requirement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: Real-time tracking is a concrete capability — a Functional solution requirement.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Intermediate', "question": "'Grow share in the SMB segment by 10% within two years.' This is BEST classified as:", "options": ['A) Business requirement', 'B) Stakeholder requirement', 'C) Functional solution requirement', 'D) Non-functional solution requirement'], "answer": 'A) Business requirement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: A strategic growth objective is a Business requirement.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Foundational', "question": "'The system must let users upload PDF documents up to 50 MB.' This is BEST classified as:", "options": ['A) Business requirement', 'B) Stakeholder requirement', 'C) Functional solution requirement', 'D) Non-functional solution requirement'], "answer": 'C) Functional solution requirement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: PDF upload with a size limit is a concrete capability — a Functional solution requirement.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Foundational', "question": "'The system must be available on the App Store for iOS and Android.' This is BEST classified as:", "options": ['A) Business requirement', 'B) Stakeholder requirement', 'C) Functional solution requirement', 'D) Non-functional solution requirement'], "answer": 'D) Non-functional solution requirement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: Platform availability is a technical constraint — a Non-functional solution requirement.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Intermediate', "question": "'Reduce time-to-hire from 45 to 20 days.' This is BEST classified as:", "options": ['A) Business requirement', 'B) Stakeholder requirement', 'C) Functional solution requirement', 'D) Non-functional solution requirement'], "answer": 'A) Business requirement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: An organizational efficiency goal is a Business requirement.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Intermediate', "question": "'As an account manager, I need to see all customer interactions in a single view.' This is BEST classified as:", "options": ['A) Business requirement', 'B) Stakeholder requirement', 'C) Functional solution requirement', 'D) Non-functional solution requirement'], "answer": 'B) Stakeholder requirement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: A need from a specific role is a Stakeholder requirement.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Intermediate', "question": "'The system must automatically flag transactions lacking the required tax documentation.' This is BEST classified as:", "options": ['A) Business requirement', 'B) Stakeholder requirement', 'C) Functional solution requirement', 'D) Non-functional solution requirement'], "answer": 'C) Functional solution requirement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: Automatic flagging is a system behavior — a Functional solution requirement.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Foundational', "question": "'The system must comply with GDPR in its data processing.' This is BEST classified as:", "options": ['A) Business requirement', 'B) Stakeholder requirement', 'C) Functional solution requirement', 'D) Non-functional solution requirement'], "answer": 'D) Non-functional solution requirement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: GDPR compliance is a quality constraint — a Non-functional solution requirement.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Foundational', "question": 'Into how many categories does BABOK® v3 classify requirements?', "options": ['A) 3', 'B) 4', 'C) 5', 'D) 6'], "answer": 'B) 4', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: The four categories are Business, Stakeholder, Solution (functional + non-functional) and Transition.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Foundational', "question": 'Solution requirements are subdivided into:', "options": ['A) Functional and Non-functional', 'B) Explicit and Implicit', 'C) Transition and Operational', 'D) Business and Stakeholder'], "answer": 'A) Functional and Non-functional', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: Solution requirements consist of Functional ones (behaviors) and Non-functional ones (quality attributes).'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Foundational', "question": 'Transition requirements are BEST described as:', "options": ['A) Capabilities needed only to move from the current state to the future state', 'B) Strategic business objectives', 'C) Permanent capabilities after go-live', 'D) User expectations about the final system'], "answer": 'A) Capabilities needed only to move from the current state to the future state', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: Transition requirements are temporary — they support the cutover and are retired once the transition is complete.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Intermediate', "question": "A stakeholder asks: 'After go-live, will we still need the data migration requirement?' The CORRECT answer is:", "options": ['A) Only if the sponsor decides so', 'B) Yes, it becomes a permanent non-functional one', 'C) No, transition requirements are temporary and are retired once the transition is complete', 'D) Yes, it becomes a stakeholder requirement'], "answer": 'C) No, transition requirements are temporary and are retired once the transition is complete', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: Transition requirements are explicitly temporary — needed to reach the future state, not beyond it.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Advanced', "question": "A BA challenges their team: 'Show me how each solution requirement traces to a stakeholder or business requirement.' The MAIN purpose is:", "options": ['A) Increase documentation volume', 'B) Satisfy audit', 'C) Ensure that every solution requirement has legitimate justification tied to real needs', 'D) Slow down development'], "answer": 'C) Ensure that every solution requirement has legitimate justification tied to real needs', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: Traceability ensures there are no orphaned or unjustified requirements in the baseline.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Advanced', "question": "A requirement states: 'The system must be intuitive and easy to use.' A BA flags it as inadequate because:", "options": ['A) It is vague and not measurable; a valid non-functional requirement must specify measurable criteria such as task completion rate or error frequency', 'B) Usability is always a transition requirement', 'C) Usability is not a valid type', 'D) It is a design specification'], "answer": 'A) It is vague and not measurable; a valid non-functional requirement must specify measurable criteria such as task completion rate or error frequency', "explanation": "BABOK® v3 §2.2: Non-functional requirements must be measurable and verifiable; 'intuitive' cannot be verified without concrete criteria."},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Expert', "question": 'The four requirement categories form a hierarchical relationship. The MOST precise description is:', "options": ['A) Business requirements set the intent; stakeholder requirements translate it into group needs; solution requirements define how to satisfy them; transition requirements address temporary needs — each traces up to the level above', 'B) The hierarchy only applies to waterfall', 'C) They are not hierarchical; they are independent', 'D) Solution ones are the most important'], "answer": 'A) Business requirements set the intent; stakeholder requirements translate it into group needs; solution requirements define how to satisfy them; transition requirements address temporary needs — each traces up to the level above', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: The four types form a traceability hierarchy: Business → Stakeholder → Solution, with Transition in parallel.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.3 Stakeholders | Foundational', "question": 'External customers who buy and use the product BEST represent:', "options": ['A) Tester', 'B) Customer', 'C) Supplier', 'D) Operational Support'], "answer": 'B) Customer', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.3: Those who buy or receive value from the solution are Customer stakeholders.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.3 Stakeholders | Foundational', "question": 'Which statement about stakeholder identification is TRUE per BABOK® v3?', "options": ['A) It is an ongoing activity; stakeholders may be added, removed or change throughout the initiative', 'B) It is completed once at the start and not revisited', 'C) Only internal ones are identified', 'D) Only those with formal authority'], "answer": 'A) It is an ongoing activity; stakeholders may be added, removed or change throughout the initiative', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.3: Stakeholder identification and analysis are ongoing; the landscape evolves throughout the initiative.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.3 Stakeholders | Foundational', "question": 'A team member who writes code for the solution BEST represents:', "options": ['A) Operational Support', 'B) Domain SME', 'C) End User', 'D) Supplier'], "answer": 'D) Supplier', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.3: Developers who build the solution are considered Suppliers — they provide a product/service that supports the change.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.3 Stakeholders | Intermediate', "question": 'A BA uses a RACI matrix to plan engagement. This tool defines:', "options": ['A) The budget', 'B) Technical design decisions', 'C) Requirements priorities', 'D) Who is Responsible, Accountable, Consulted and Informed for each activity'], "answer": 'D) Who is Responsible, Accountable, Consulted and Informed for each activity', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.3: A RACI matrix clarifies stakeholder roles in BA activities, avoiding ambiguity.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.3 Stakeholders | Intermediate', "question": "A formally 'informed only' stakeholder has strong informal influence over the decision-maker. The MOST appropriate action is:", "options": ['A) Follow the formal designation and limit engagement', 'B) Recognize the informal influence and adjust the engagement strategy', 'C) Escalate them to HR', 'D) Remove them from the register'], "answer": 'B) Recognize the informal influence and adjust the engagement strategy', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.3: Formal authority and informal influence differ; the BA must plan for both.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.3 Stakeholders | Intermediate', "question": 'An SME contributes rich knowledge in 1-on-1 interviews but stays silent in group workshops. The MOST appropriate response is:', "options": ['A) Use individual techniques with this SME and group techniques for other purposes, adapting the technique to the individual preference', 'B) Replace them with someone more outspoken', 'C) Exclude them from the workshops', 'D) Force their participation in the group'], "answer": 'A) Use individual techniques with this SME and group techniques for other purposes, adapting the technique to the individual preference', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.3: Effective engagement recognizes individual characteristics and adapts the choice of techniques.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.3 Stakeholders | Advanced', "question": "An initiative will automate front-line workers' jobs. The BA MUST plan for:", "options": ['A) Exclude those affected to avoid resistance', 'B) Guarantee that no jobs will be cut', 'C) Intensive, transparent engagement with those affected, capturing their operational knowledge and incorporating their concerns', 'D) Leave all communication to HR'], "answer": 'C) Intensive, transparent engagement with those affected, capturing their operational knowledge and incorporating their concerns', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.3: Affected employees have critical knowledge and legitimate concerns; transparent engagement is the BABOK®-aligned approach.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.3 Stakeholders | Advanced', "question": 'A BA identifies 12 groups but has limited time for elicitation. The prioritization MOST aligned with BABOK® v3 is:', "options": ['A) Prioritize the most vocal ones', 'B) Prioritize by seniority', 'C) Prioritize by already-documented requirements', 'D) Prioritize by stakeholder analysis (influence, proximity to the Need, risk of omission) without entirely excluding any group'], "answer": 'D) Prioritize by stakeholder analysis (influence, proximity to the Need, risk of omission) without entirely excluding any group', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.3: Engagement prioritization uses analytical criteria — influence, interest, proximity to the Need, and risk of exclusion.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.3 Stakeholders | Expert', "question": "A BA mentors a junior who asks: 'Why do we include the BA themselves on the stakeholder list?' The answer MOST aligned with BABOK® v3 is:", "options": ["A) The BA has a relationship with the change and its outcomes; acknowledging this allows transparency about one's own biases or interests", 'B) The BA manages everyone, so they come first', 'C) It is only required in regulated industries', 'D) It is a formality with no genuine stake'], "answer": "A) The BA has a relationship with the change and its outcomes; acknowledging this allows transparency about one's own biases or interests", "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.3: Recognizing the BA as a stakeholder promotes transparency and professional objectivity.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.4 Requirements vs Designs | Foundational', "question": "A use case that describes behavior from the user's perspective without specifying implementation is BEST classified as:", "options": ['A) Transition requirement', 'B) Design artifact', 'C) Requirements artifact', 'D) Non-functional requirement'], "answer": 'C) Requirements artifact', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.4: A use case that defines behavior without implementation is a requirements artifact.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.4 Requirements vs Designs | Foundational', "question": 'Per BABOK® v3, designs are described as:', "options": ['A) The required capabilities of the solution', 'B) Usable representations of a solution that address stakeholder needs', 'C) The strategic goals', 'D) What the organization needs and why'], "answer": 'B) Usable representations of a solution that address stakeholder needs', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.4: Designs are usable representations of a solution — they describe how it will be structured and how it will work.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.4 Requirements vs Designs | Foundational', "question": 'An entity-relationship diagram (ERD) of the data model BEST represents:', "options": ['A) A design artifact that describes the data structure', 'B) A business requirement', 'C) A stakeholder requirement', 'D) A non-functional requirement'], "answer": 'A) A design artifact that describes the data structure', "explanation": "BABOK® v3 §2.4: An ERD defines the solution's data structure — it is a design artifact."},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.4 Requirements vs Designs | Intermediate', "question": 'A BA writes test cases with specific data, expected DB states and exact UI field values. These are BEST classified as:', "options": ['A) Functional requirements', 'B) Non-functional requirements', 'C) Design-level artifacts that specify how the testing will be carried out', 'D) Business requirements'], "answer": 'C) Design-level artifacts that specify how the testing will be carried out', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.4: Detailed test cases with data and expected states describe how the testing will be done — they are design-level.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.4 Requirements vs Designs | Intermediate', "question": "A BA documents: 'The system must let customers track their orders in real time.' This is BEST classified as:", "options": ['A) Business requirement', 'B) Functional solution requirement', 'C) Design artifact', 'D) Non-functional requirement'], "answer": 'B) Functional solution requirement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.4: Stating what the system must do without specifying how is a functional requirement.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.4 Requirements vs Designs | Advanced', "question": 'A BA hands over their requirements document directly as a design specification. What risk does this create?', "options": ['A) Scope creep', 'B) Confusing requirements (what is needed) with design (how to build it), unnecessarily constraining the solution space', 'C) Lack of stakeholder sign-off', 'D) None; they are equivalent'], "answer": 'B) Confusing requirements (what is needed) with design (how to build it), unnecessarily constraining the solution space', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.4: Treating requirements as design specifications closes off design options.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.4 Requirements vs Designs | Expert', "question": 'A BA produces a future-state process model; development treats it as a design spec. The system matches the model but fails to achieve the business outcome. The MOST precise root cause per BABOK® v3 is:', "options": ['A) The PM failed to control scope', 'B) The model was a requirements artifact used as a design spec; collapsing the boundary made the solution satisfy the model instead of the underlying Need and Value', 'C) Implementation errors', 'D) Lack of documentation'], "answer": 'B) The model was a requirements artifact used as a design spec; collapsing the boundary made the solution satisfy the model instead of the underlying Need and Value', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.4: Treating requirements artifacts as designs forces the solution to satisfy the artifact instead of the real Need.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.5 Integration | Intermediate', "question": "A stakeholder says 'I need the system to be fast'. After investigating, the BA establishes this means a load time under 1.5 s for 95% of requests. What transformation occurred?", "options": ['A) A functional requirement was rewritten as a design constraint', 'B) A business requirement became a transition requirement', 'C) A vague stakeholder need was refined into a measurable non-functional requirement', 'D) A stakeholder requirement was elevated to a business one'], "answer": 'C) A vague stakeholder need was refined into a measurable non-functional requirement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: Clarifying a vague quality expectation into a measurable criterion produces a non-functional solution requirement.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.5 Integration | Intermediate', "question": 'The requirements were verified as complete, consistent and unambiguous, but the solution does not improve business performance. The MOST likely point of failure is:', "options": ['A) The PM failed to deliver', 'B) Verification was insufficient', 'C) Development quality was poor', 'D) The requirements were verified (internally correct) but not validated (connected to the real Need and Value)'], "answer": 'D) The requirements were verified (internally correct) but not validated (connected to the real Need and Value)', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: Verification checks internal quality; validation checks that they will lead to solutions that deliver Value. Both are necessary.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.5 Integration | Advanced', "question": 'A BA maps a regulatory compliance need to a solution requirement, which traces to a system design. This chain BEST illustrates:', "options": ['A) Traceability — the RLCM mechanism that ensures every solution element is linked to a justified need', 'B) The difference between BA and PM', 'C) The BACCM in action', 'D) The sequential nature of the knowledge areas'], "answer": 'A) Traceability — the RLCM mechanism that ensures every solution element is linked to a justified need', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: Traceability connects needs → requirements → designs, providing justification and impact-analysis capability.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.5 Integration | Advanced', "question": "A BA is challenged: 'Your document is 200 pages; let's skip the business and stakeholder requirements and give development only the functional ones.' The reply MOST aligned with BABOK® v3 is:", "options": ['A) Accept: only functional and non-functional matter', "B) The real problem is the document's length", 'C) Accept: the functional ones are enough for development', 'D) Business and stakeholder requirements provide the context and justification; without them, development cannot make sound decisions when requirements conflict or are ambiguous'], "answer": 'D) Business and stakeholder requirements provide the context and justification; without them, development cannot make sound decisions when requirements conflict or are ambiguous', "explanation": "BABOK® v3 §2.2: Higher-level requirements provide the 'why', essential when interpreting ambiguous or conflicting functional ones."},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.5 Integration | Expert', "question": 'The examiner asks which represents the MOST complete application of the Chapter 2 concepts at initiation. The answer is:', "options": ['A) Produce a business case and project charter', 'B) Examine Need (the right problem?), identify Stakeholders, define Value by group, assess Context, validate that the Solution addresses the Need, and confirm the scope of the Change', 'C) Select the platform according to standards', 'D) Document all functional requirements before talking to stakeholders'], "answer": 'B) Examine Need (the right problem?), identify Stakeholders, define Value by group, assess Context, validate that the Solution addresses the Need, and confirm the scope of the Change', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.1: Expert application of the BACCM at initiation integrates the six concepts as analytical questions, not sequential steps.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.1 BACCM | Foundational', "question": "In the BACCM, 'Value' is BEST described as:", "options": ['A) The worth, importance or usefulness of something to a stakeholder within a context', 'B) The technical quality metrics', 'C) Only the financial return', 'D) The number of requirements delivered'], "answer": 'A) The worth, importance or usefulness of something to a stakeholder within a context', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.1: Value is specific to each stakeholder and context-dependent; it is not purely financial.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.1 BACCM | Foundational', "question": "In the BACCM, 'Solution' refers to:", "options": ['A) A contract deliverable', 'B) Always a software system', 'C) A specific way to satisfy one or more needs within a context', 'D) The technical architecture of the future state'], "answer": 'C) A specific way to satisfy one or more needs within a context', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.1: A Solution is the chosen approach to satisfy needs — it can be a process, product, service or system.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.1 BACCM | Intermediate', "question": 'An organization deploys a new CRM to improve sales. The CRM is BEST classified as:', "options": ['A) Value', 'B) Change', 'C) Solution', 'D) Need'], "answer": 'C) Solution', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.1: The CRM — the specific way to satisfy the sales need — is a Solution.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.1 BACCM | Intermediate', "question": 'The improvement in close rate and revenue growth from the CRM is BEST classified as:', "options": ['A) Change', 'B) Solution', 'C) Need', 'D) Value'], "answer": 'D) Value', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.1: The business benefit delivered to stakeholders is Value.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.1 BACCM | Advanced', "question": 'An organization launches a transformation without defining what Value it should deliver. The MOST significant risk is:', "options": ["A) Development won't know what to build", 'B) The PM will lose control', 'C) It will take too long', 'D) There will be no shared basis to measure success or make trade-off decisions'], "answer": 'D) There will be no shared basis to measure success or make trade-off decisions', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.1: Without a defined Value, the organization lacks a success criterion.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.1 BACCM | Expert', "question": "A candidate is asked: 'How does the BACCM help when two stakeholders define success in completely different ways?' The MOST complete answer is:", "options": ['A) The BACCM does not address stakeholder conflicts', 'B) The BA picks a definition and moves on', 'C) The Value concept is specific to each stakeholder; the BA must map what Value means for each group and facilitate alignment before moving forward', 'D) Disagreements are resolved by the PM'], "answer": 'C) The Value concept is specific to each stakeholder; the BA must map what Value means for each group and facilitate alignment before moving forward', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.1: Value is specific by definition; the BA must surface and reconcile the Value definitions before they turn into requirements conflicts.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Foundational', "question": "'The new portal must let suppliers check the status of their payments.' This is BEST classified as:", "options": ['A) Business requirement', 'B) Stakeholder requirement', 'C) Functional solution requirement', 'D) Non-functional solution requirement'], "answer": 'C) Functional solution requirement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: Checking payment status is a concrete system capability — a Functional solution requirement.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Foundational', "question": "'Improve the Net Promoter Score from 30 to 50 within 18 months.' This is BEST classified as:", "options": ['A) Business requirement', 'B) Stakeholder requirement', 'C) Functional solution requirement', 'D) Non-functional solution requirement'], "answer": 'A) Business requirement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: A measurable organizational goal that drives the initiative is a Business requirement.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Intermediate', "question": "'The system must log all user operations for audit for 5 years.' This is BEST classified as:", "options": ['A) Business requirement', 'B) Stakeholder requirement', 'C) Functional solution requirement', 'D) Non-functional solution requirement'], "answer": 'D) Non-functional solution requirement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: Audit logging with retention is a compliance attribute — a Non-functional solution requirement.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Intermediate', "question": "'As an internal auditor, I need to export the transaction log filtered by date and user.' This is BEST classified as:", "options": ['A) Business requirement', 'B) Stakeholder requirement', 'C) Functional solution requirement', 'D) Non-functional solution requirement'], "answer": 'B) Stakeholder requirement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: A need from a specific role is a Stakeholder requirement.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Intermediate', "question": "'All staff must have credentials in the new system before the first day of operation.' This is BEST classified as:", "options": ['A) Business requirement', 'B) Transition requirement', 'C) Functional requirement', 'D) Non-functional requirement'], "answer": 'B) Transition requirement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: Provisioning credentials for the cutover is a Transition requirement.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Advanced', "question": "A BA validates that all requirements are necessary and asks 'what would happen if we removed it?'. For which type of requirement is this question MOST important?", "options": ['A) Business: they are always necessary', 'B) Any requirement without clear upward traceability — confirming its necessity requires tracing it to a stakeholder or objective', 'C) Non-functional: they are optional', 'D) Transition ones: they must always be kept'], "answer": 'B) Any requirement without clear upward traceability — confirming its necessity requires tracing it to a stakeholder or objective', "explanation": "BABOK® v3 §2.2: A requirement that does not trace to a business objective or stakeholder may be unnecessary; the 'remove it' test surfaces orphans."},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Advanced', "question": "A business case states 'reduce time-to-hire from 45 to 20 days' and then it is elicited that 'HR leads need to post vacancies to several portals at once'. These two BEST illustrate:", "options": ['A) Two business requirements of differing specificity', 'B) Two functional requirements', 'C) A Business requirement (goal) and a Stakeholder requirement (concrete need) that supports it', 'D) A Business and a Transition requirement'], "answer": 'C) A Business requirement (goal) and a Stakeholder requirement (concrete need) that supports it', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: Business requirements state goals; stakeholder requirements describe how groups need to work to achieve them. Each traces to the other.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Expert', "question": "A BA asks whether non-functional requirements are optional when the sponsor doesn't explicitly request them. The answer MOST aligned with BABOK® v3 is:", "options": ['A) Only the performance ones are mandatory', "B) Yes, if they aren't requested they aren't needed", 'C) Yes, only regulated industries require them', 'D) No; security, performance and availability are often implicit expectations that must be captured explicitly even when stakeholders do not articulate them'], "answer": 'D) No; security, performance and availability are often implicit expectations that must be captured explicitly even when stakeholders do not articulate them', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: Many non-functional requirements are assumed, unstated expectations; the BA must identify them proactively.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.3 Stakeholders | Foundational', "question": 'Per BABOK® v3, a stakeholder is BEST defined as:', "options": ['A) The sponsor and the steering committee', 'B) Only the direct users of the solution', 'C) A group or individual with a relationship to the change, the need or the solution', 'D) Only internal employees'], "answer": 'C) A group or individual with a relationship to the change, the need or the solution', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.3: Stakeholders are defined broadly — any party with a relationship to the change, need or solution.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.3 Stakeholders | Foundational', "question": 'Which statement about stakeholders is TRUE per BABOK® v3?', "options": ['A) Must have a formal role in the project', "B) Must be a person; groups don't count", 'C) Must be positively affected', 'D) It can be any group or individual with a relationship to the change, need or solution — even those negatively affected'], "answer": 'D) It can be any group or individual with a relationship to the change, need or solution — even those negatively affected', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.3: The definition is broad and inclusive — a formal role, positive impact, or being an individual are not requirements.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.3 Stakeholders | Intermediate', "question": 'A stakeholder who previously supported the initiative now rejects it due to a restructuring. The MOST appropriate response is:', "options": ['A) Remove them from the register', 'B) Update the analysis to reflect the changed attitude and revise the engagement strategy', 'C) Escalate immediately to the committee', 'D) Continue without adjusting; attitudes are temporary'], "answer": 'B) Update the analysis to reflect the changed attitude and revise the engagement strategy', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.3: Attitudes change; the BA must update the analysis and adapt the strategy when this is detected.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.3 Stakeholders | Intermediate', "question": 'A workshop is attended only by senior stakeholders; front-line users are not. Requirements are approved and usability problems then emerge. This BEST illustrates:', "options": ['A) Senior sign-off is enough', 'B) Usability testing was insufficient', 'C) Incomplete engagement: front-line users have critical requirements that senior staff cannot represent well', 'D) Development misinterpreted the requirements'], "answer": 'C) Incomplete engagement: front-line users have critical requirements that senior staff cannot represent well', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.3: Different stakeholder types have different knowledge; senior staff do not substitute for front-line users.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.3 Stakeholders | Intermediate', "question": 'The factor that MOST increases stakeholder complexity is:', "options": ['A) Stakeholders all fully supportive', 'B) Stakeholders that are geographically dispersed, with conflicting interests, diverse cultures and varying availability', 'C) Stakeholders that are all senior executives', 'D) Few stakeholders in a single department'], "answer": 'B) Stakeholders that are geographically dispersed, with conflicting interests, diverse cultures and varying availability', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.3: Multiple dimensions of difference — geographic, cultural, of interests, of availability — increase complexity.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.3 Stakeholders | Advanced', "question": 'Two executive sponsors with equal authority have conflicting visions. The MOST appropriate action by the BA is:', "options": ['A) Pick one vision and document it as official', 'B) Facilitate a structured discussion to surface the conflict, assess alignment with the agreed Need and Value, and escalate to governance if there is no agreement', 'C) Let the PM resolve it', 'D) Proceed according to the most recent vision'], "answer": 'B) Facilitate a structured discussion to surface the conflict, assess alignment with the agreed Need and Value, and escalate to governance if there is no agreement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.3: Conflicting sponsor visions are resolved through structured facilitation anchored in the Need and Value, not by choosing unilaterally.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.3 Stakeholders | Advanced', "question": 'For a system that replaces manual data entry across three departments, the BA MUST include as stakeholders:', "options": ['A) Only the department heads who approved the project', 'B) Only the IT team that will build it', 'C) Only those who attended the kick-off', 'D) Front-line staff who do the data entry, their supervisors, IT, downstream teams that use the data, and regulators who audit it'], "answer": 'D) Front-line staff who do the data entry, their supervisors, IT, downstream teams that use the data, and regulators who audit it', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.3: Complete identification includes all parties with a relationship to the change: those affected, users of the outputs, and compliance overseers.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.3 Stakeholders | Expert', "question": 'A BA manages 47 stakeholder groups and the program director fears paralysis. The resolution MOST aligned with BABOK® v3 is:', "options": ['A) Engage only those who attend meetings', 'B) Delegate all engagement to the PM', 'C) Apply stakeholder analysis to define differentiated engagement strategies: high-impact groups get intensive engagement and others appropriate communication', 'D) Reduce the list to the 10 most senior'], "answer": 'C) Apply stakeholder analysis to define differentiated engagement strategies: high-impact groups get intensive engagement and others appropriate communication', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.3: Stakeholder analysis produces differentiated strategies, not identical ones.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.4 Requirements vs Designs | Foundational', "question": "'The system must send an automatic confirmation email when an order is placed.' This is BEST classified as:", "options": ['A) Business requirement', 'B) Non-functional requirement', 'C) Functional solution requirement', 'D) Design specification'], "answer": 'C) Functional solution requirement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.4: Stating what the system must do without prescribing how is a functional requirement.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.4 Requirements vs Designs | Foundational', "question": 'A developer produces a class diagram with relationships and methods. This BEST represents:', "options": ['A) A stakeholder requirement', 'B) A design artifact', 'C) A functional requirement', 'D) A non-functional requirement'], "answer": 'B) A design artifact', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.4: A class diagram specifies how the solution will be built — it is a design artifact.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.4 Requirements vs Designs | Intermediate', "question": 'A product owner writes acceptance criteria with exact UI element names and pixel layout. Per BABOK® v3 these MOST represent:', "options": ['A) Transition requirements', 'B) Design constraints embedded in acceptance criteria', 'C) Non-functional requirements', 'D) Pure stakeholder requirements'], "answer": 'B) Design constraints embedded in acceptance criteria', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.4: When acceptance criteria prescribe implementation details, they cross into design territory.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.4 Requirements vs Designs | Advanced', "question": "A team asks: 'Can we use your requirements document as a design spec?' The answer MOST aligned with BABOK® v3 is:", "options": ['A) No; requirements define what is needed and design how to meet it; mixing them unnecessarily constrains the solution space', 'B) Yes, but only for functional ones', 'C) Let the PM decide', 'D) Yes; requirements and design are equivalent'], "answer": 'A) No; requirements define what is needed and design how to meet it; mixing them unnecessarily constrains the solution space', "explanation": "BABOK® v3 §2.4: Using requirements as a design spec removes the team's flexibility to find optimal approaches."},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.4 Requirements vs Designs | Expert', "question": 'When does an artifact shift from being a requirement to being a design? The MOST precise answer per BABOK® v3 is:', "options": ['A) Designs are always created by architects and requirements by BAs', 'B) The boundary is defined by level of abstraction and intent: requirements are solution-agnostic (what/why); designs describe how a specific solution will be structured; the same artifact can cross the boundary', 'C) When the sponsor approves it', 'D) When the document passes from the BA to the developer'], "answer": 'B) The boundary is defined by level of abstraction and intent: requirements are solution-agnostic (what/why); designs describe how a specific solution will be structured; the same artifact can cross the boundary', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.4: The distinction is not by role or document, but by abstraction and intent.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.5 Integration | Foundational', "question": 'Using the Chapter 2 concepts, which activity must precede writing functional requirements?', "options": ['A) Identify stakeholders and understand the Need and the Context', 'B) Design the architecture', 'C) Develop test cases', 'D) Select the technology'], "answer": 'A) Identify stakeholders and understand the Need and the Context', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.1, §2.3: Understanding stakeholders, Need and Context precedes defining functional requirements.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.5 Integration | Intermediate', "question": 'Two stakeholders have conflicting requirements for the same feature. The BA facilitates and one is modified, which must now be re-examined for impact on related requirements. This relates BEST to:', "options": ['A) Business requirements classification', 'B) The BACCM Value concept', 'C) Requirements traceability and impact analysis', 'D) Stakeholder influence mapping'], "answer": 'C) Requirements traceability and impact analysis', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: Traceability allows identifying all requirements affected when one changes, enabling impact analysis.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.5 Integration | Advanced', "question": 'A BA prepares an executive briefing on why a newly delivered system underperforms. They structure their analysis with the BACCM. The sequence of questions that BEST reflects a BACCM diagnosis is:', "options": ['A) Was it on time? On budget? Were there defects?', 'B) Was the Need correctly identified? Was the Context understood? Were the right Stakeholders engaged? Was Value defined in terms each group recognizes? Was the Solution validated against the Need?', 'C) Were all requirements approved? Did testers participate? Was UAT completed?', 'D) Did the PM escalate the issues? Were the changes processed well?'], "answer": 'B) Was the Need correctly identified? Was the Context understood? Were the right Stakeholders engaged? Was Value defined in terms each group recognizes? Was the Solution validated against the Need?', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.1: A BACCM diagnosis examines each concept in turn — not delivery metrics — to find the root cause of the value shortfall.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.5 Integration | Expert', "question": 'Post-delivery analysis: benefits 30% below target. The BACCM shows a correct Need, monitored Context, engaged Stakeholders and a Solution delivered as designed, yet Value below target. The MOST likely remaining explanation is:', "options": ['A) The Solution, even though well built, does not deliver the expected Value in the real operating Context; the Value definition assumed conditions that did not materialize', 'B) Documentation was missing', 'C) The PM failed to manage scope', 'D) Development did not follow the specifications'], "answer": 'A) The Solution, even though well built, does not deliver the expected Value in the real operating Context; the Value definition assumed conditions that did not materialize', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.1: Value realization depends on real conditions; a correct Solution can underperform if the Value model assumed Context factors that did not materialize.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.1 BACCM | Foundational', "question": "In the BACCM, which concept answers the question 'what problem or opportunity exists?'", "options": ['A) Solution', 'B) Need', 'C) Change', 'D) Value'], "answer": 'B) Need', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.1: The Need represents the problem to be solved or the opportunity to be captured.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.1 BACCM | Foundational', "question": 'A university digitizes its enrollment process. The new enrollment portal is, in the BACCM:', "options": ['A) Need', 'B) Context', 'C) Solution', 'D) Value'], "answer": 'C) Solution', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.1: The portal is the specific way chosen to satisfy the need — a Solution.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.1 BACCM | Foundational', "question": 'The saving of 2,000 administrative hours per year after digitizing enrollment is, in the BACCM:', "options": ['A) Value', 'B) Need', 'C) Change', 'D) Context'], "answer": 'A) Value', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.1: The measurable benefit delivered is Value.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.1 BACCM | Foundational', "question": 'The students who will use the enrollment portal are, in the BACCM:', "options": ['A) Context', 'B) Stakeholder', 'C) Need', 'D) Solution'], "answer": 'B) Stakeholder', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.1: Those with a relationship to the change or the solution are Stakeholders.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.1 BACCM | Foundational', "question": 'The regional education laws that constrain the portal are, in the BACCM:', "options": ['A) Need', 'B) Solution', 'C) Context', 'D) Value'], "answer": 'C) Context', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.1: The legal framework is part of the Context.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.1 BACCM | Intermediate', "question": 'A retailer cuts its physical catalog to drive online sales but loses older customers who preferred the catalog. This BEST illustrates that:', "options": ['A) The Need was poorly defined', 'B) Value is specific to each stakeholder group', 'C) The Context did not exist', 'D) There was no Change'], "answer": 'B) Value is specific to each stakeholder group', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.1: The same change delivers different Value to different stakeholder groups.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.1 BACCM | Intermediate', "question": 'Before committing to a software solution, a BA insists on documenting the real problem. They are prioritizing:', "options": ['A) Solution', 'B) Need', 'C) Change', 'D) Value'], "answer": 'B) Need', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.1: Establishing the Need precedes selecting the Solution.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.1 BACCM | Intermediate', "question": 'A new tax policy changes mid-way through a billing project. In the BACCM, what changed primarily is:', "options": ['A) Value', 'B) Context', 'C) Solution', 'D) Stakeholder'], "answer": 'B) Context', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.1: An external regulatory change modifies the Context.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.1 BACCM | Advanced', "question": 'A project meets all its requirements but stakeholders feel it solved nothing. The MOST likely BACCM gap is:', "options": ["A) Value was never defined from the stakeholders' perspective", 'B) Testers were missing', 'C) The schedule was short', 'D) The budget was exceeded'], "answer": "A) Value was never defined from the stakeholders' perspective", "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.1: Without Value defined by the stakeholders, technical and perceived success diverge.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.1 BACCM | Advanced', "question": 'A BA uses the BACCM as a post-mortem for a failed project: the solution was correct but for the wrong problem. The failure is:', "options": ['A) Solution–Need misalignment', 'B) Value–Context misalignment', 'C) Change–Stakeholder conflict', 'D) Context–Solution conflict'], "answer": 'A) Solution–Need misalignment', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.1: Building correct solutions for poorly defined needs is the Solution–Need misalignment.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.1 BACCM | Expert', "question": 'An agile team claims its sprint ceremonies replace BACCM analysis. The MOST precise rebuttal is:', "options": ['A) The BACCM is purely theoretical', 'B) Agree: the ceremonies cover everything', 'C) Sprint artifacts name concepts but do not guarantee rigorous analysis; a sprint goal can represent the wrong Need and a Definition of Done can measure delivery instead of Value', 'D) The BACCM only applies to waterfall'], "answer": 'C) Sprint artifacts name concepts but do not guarantee rigorous analysis; a sprint goal can represent the wrong Need and a Definition of Done can measure delivery instead of Value', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.1: Ceremonies provide cadence; the BACCM provides analytical depth. Naming concepts is no substitute for examining them.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Foundational', "question": "'Reduce customer complaints by 25% within one year.' This is BEST classified as:", "options": ['A) Business requirement', 'B) Stakeholder requirement', 'C) Functional solution requirement', 'D) Non-functional solution requirement'], "answer": 'A) Business requirement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: An organizational improvement goal is a Business requirement.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Foundational', "question": "'The system must allow generating a printable receipt after each payment.' This is BEST classified as:", "options": ['A) Business requirement', 'B) Stakeholder requirement', 'C) Functional solution requirement', 'D) Non-functional solution requirement'], "answer": 'C) Functional solution requirement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: Generating a receipt is a concrete capability — a Functional solution requirement.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Foundational', "question": "'The system must support two-factor authentication.' This is BEST classified as:", "options": ['A) Business requirement', 'B) Stakeholder requirement', 'C) Functional solution requirement', 'D) Non-functional solution requirement'], "answer": 'D) Non-functional solution requirement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: Two-factor authentication is a security attribute — a Non-functional solution requirement.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Foundational', "question": "'As a cashier, I need to void a sale the same day with supervisor authorization.' This is BEST classified as:", "options": ['A) Business requirement', 'B) Stakeholder requirement', 'C) Functional solution requirement', 'D) Non-functional solution requirement'], "answer": 'B) Stakeholder requirement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: A need from a specific role is a Stakeholder requirement.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Intermediate', "question": "'The system must support 200,000 registered users without degrading performance.' This is BEST classified as:", "options": ['A) Business requirement', 'B) Stakeholder requirement', 'C) Functional solution requirement', 'D) Non-functional solution requirement'], "answer": 'D) Non-functional solution requirement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: Scalability is a non-functional attribute — a Non-functional solution requirement.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Intermediate', "question": "'The system must allow scheduling recurring reports by email.' This is BEST classified as:", "options": ['A) Business requirement', 'B) Stakeholder requirement', 'C) Functional solution requirement', 'D) Non-functional solution requirement'], "answer": 'C) Functional solution requirement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: Scheduling reports is a concrete capability — a Functional solution requirement.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Intermediate', "question": "'As compliance officer, I need an immutable record of configuration changes.' This is BEST classified as:", "options": ['A) Business requirement', 'B) Stakeholder requirement', 'C) Functional solution requirement', 'D) Non-functional solution requirement'], "answer": 'B) Stakeholder requirement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: A need from a specific role is a Stakeholder requirement.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Intermediate', "question": "'The system must meet WCAG 2.1 level AA accessibility standards.' This is BEST classified as:", "options": ['A) Business requirement', 'B) Stakeholder requirement', 'C) Functional solution requirement', 'D) Non-functional solution requirement'], "answer": 'D) Non-functional solution requirement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: Accessibility is a quality attribute — a Non-functional solution requirement.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Intermediate', "question": "'The system must allow exporting data to CSV and Excel.' This is BEST classified as:", "options": ['A) Business requirement', 'B) Stakeholder requirement', 'C) Functional solution requirement', 'D) Non-functional solution requirement'], "answer": 'C) Functional solution requirement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: Data export is a concrete capability — a Functional solution requirement.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Foundational', "question": "'Position the brand as the sector's sustainability leader within 3 years.' This is BEST classified as:", "options": ['A) Business requirement', 'B) Stakeholder requirement', 'C) Functional solution requirement', 'D) Non-functional solution requirement'], "answer": 'A) Business requirement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: An organizational strategic objective is a Business requirement.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Intermediate', "question": "'Employee data from the old system must be anonymized before the test migration.' This is BEST classified as:", "options": ['A) Business requirement', 'B) Transition requirement', 'C) Functional requirement', 'D) Non-functional requirement'], "answer": 'B) Transition requirement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: Preparation needed only for the migration is a Transition requirement.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Intermediate', "question": "'The old and new systems must run in parallel for one month after go-live.' This is BEST classified as:", "options": ['A) Business requirement', 'B) Transition requirement', 'C) Functional requirement', 'D) Non-functional requirement'], "answer": 'B) Transition requirement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: Parallel operation during the cutover is a Transition requirement.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Foundational', "question": "'The system must send a push notification when an order changes status.' This is BEST classified as:", "options": ['A) Business requirement', 'B) Stakeholder requirement', 'C) Functional solution requirement', 'D) Non-functional solution requirement'], "answer": 'C) Functional solution requirement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: A push notification on an event is a capability — a Functional solution requirement.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Foundational', "question": "'The system must recover from a failure within 15 minutes (RTO).' This is BEST classified as:", "options": ['A) Business requirement', 'B) Stakeholder requirement', 'C) Functional solution requirement', 'D) Non-functional solution requirement'], "answer": 'D) Non-functional solution requirement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: The recovery time objective is a reliability attribute — a Non-functional solution requirement.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Intermediate', "question": "'As an administrator, I need to define roles and permissions per user.' This is BEST classified as:", "options": ['A) Business requirement', 'B) Stakeholder requirement', 'C) Functional solution requirement', 'D) Non-functional solution requirement'], "answer": 'B) Stakeholder requirement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: A need from a specific role is a Stakeholder requirement.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Intermediate', "question": "'Reduce staff turnover from 18% to 10% within two years.' This is BEST classified as:", "options": ['A) Business requirement', 'B) Stakeholder requirement', 'C) Functional solution requirement', 'D) Non-functional solution requirement'], "answer": 'A) Business requirement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: An organizational HR goal is a Business requirement.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Foundational', "question": 'Business requirements BEST describe:', "options": ['A) Temporary capabilities for the cutover', 'B) What each stakeholder needs', 'C) The high-level goals and objectives of the organization that drive the initiative', 'D) How to technically build the system'], "answer": 'C) The high-level goals and objectives of the organization that drive the initiative', "explanation": "BABOK® v3 §2.2: Business requirements capture the strategic goals — the 'why' of the initiative."},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Foundational', "question": 'Functional solution requirements describe:', "options": ['A) Strategic goals', 'B) Temporary cutover needs', 'C) The behaviors and capabilities the solution must have', 'D) Quality attributes such as performance and security'], "answer": 'C) The behaviors and capabilities the solution must have', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: Functional requirements define what the solution must do.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Foundational', "question": 'Which is NOT a BABOK® v3 requirement category?', "options": ['A) Stakeholder requirements', 'B) Transition requirements', 'C) Business requirements', 'D) Design requirements'], "answer": 'D) Design requirements', "explanation": "BABOK® v3 §2.2: The categories are Business, Stakeholder, Solution and Transition; 'Design requirements' is not one."},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Intermediate', "question": "A BA receives: 'We need the system to calculate VAT automatically.' Without further detail, this MOST likely represents:", "options": ['A) A non-functional requirement', 'B) A business requirement', 'C) A transition requirement', 'D) A stakeholder requirement that needs elaboration to become functional'], "answer": 'D) A stakeholder requirement that needs elaboration to become functional', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: A need expressed by the stakeholder must be elaborated before it becomes a functional requirement.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Advanced', "question": 'A company changes payment provider mid-project, invalidating several integration requirements. The BA must FIRST:', "options": ['A) Delete the affected requirements', 'B) Use traceability to identify all impacted requirements and assess the change before acting', 'C) Ask development to improvise', 'D) Ignore the change until closure'], "answer": 'B) Use traceability to identify all impacted requirements and assess the change before acting', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: Traceability enables impact analysis by identifying the requirements affected by a change.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Advanced', "question": "A colleague says: 'The stakeholders already approved everything, why verify?' The BEST answer is:", "options": ['A) Verification and validation are the same', 'B) Approval confirms stakeholder agreement; verification checks that the requirements are internally complete and correct, regardless of approval', 'C) Verification is only done in regulated industries', 'D) The signed approval must be accepted'], "answer": 'B) Approval confirms stakeholder agreement; verification checks that the requirements are internally complete and correct, regardless of approval', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: Verification concerns documentation quality; approval concerns consensus. They are distinct questions.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Expert', "question": 'A post-project review finds a permanent operational process documented only as a transition requirement and archived at go-live. The BEST diagnosis is:', "options": ['A) It is merely a project management gap', 'B) It was correctly classified and retired', 'C) The process was misclassified: if it persists after the transition it should have been captured as a functional or stakeholder requirement', 'D) Transition requirements should never be retired'], "answer": 'C) The process was misclassified: if it persists after the transition it should have been captured as a functional or stakeholder requirement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: Transition requirements are defined by their temporary nature; permanent activities must be solution or stakeholder requirements.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.3 Stakeholders | Foundational', "question": 'Which is a stakeholder category recognized in BABOK® v3?', "options": ['A) Only the delivery team', 'B) Only customers and users', 'C) Only management and sponsors', 'D) Sponsor, Customer, End User, Domain SME, Regulator, Supplier, Tester, Operational Support, BA, PM'], "answer": 'D) Sponsor, Customer, End User, Domain SME, Regulator, Supplier, Tester, Operational Support, BA, PM', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.3: BABOK® recognizes a broad range of internal, external and supporting stakeholder types.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.3 Stakeholders | Foundational', "question": 'An expert in accounting rules who provides domain knowledge is:', "options": ['A) Sponsor', 'B) Supplier', 'C) Customer', 'D) Domain SME'], "answer": 'D) Domain SME', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.3: Domain SMEs provide specialized knowledge of a business area.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.3 Stakeholders | Intermediate', "question": "A BA discovers that an 'informed only' stakeholder has strong informal influence over the decision-maker. They should:", "options": ['A) Follow the formal designation', 'B) Recognize the informal influence and adjust the engagement strategy', 'C) Escalate to HR', 'D) Remove them from the register'], "answer": 'B) Recognize the informal influence and adjust the engagement strategy', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.3: Formal authority and informal influence differ; the BA plans for both.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.3 Stakeholders | Intermediate', "question": 'Per BABOK® v3, who is primarily responsible for stakeholder engagement for BA activities?', "options": ['A) No single role; everyone equally', 'B) The PM exclusively', 'C) The sponsor', 'D) The BA leads engagement for BA activities, collaborating with the PM on overall management'], "answer": 'D) The BA leads engagement for BA activities, collaborating with the PM on overall management', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.3: The BA leads engagement for the BA work, in collaboration with the PM.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.3 Stakeholders | Advanced', "question": 'A key stakeholder with high influence, high interest, domain expertise and moderate resistance. The MOST complete approach is:', "options": ['A) Limit their access to the documentation', 'B) Avoid them so as not to cause disruption', 'C) Early, intensive engagement to understand the root of the resistance, leverage their expertise, address concerns transparently, and involve them in the design', 'D) Have the sponsor override their resistance'], "answer": 'C) Early, intensive engagement to understand the root of the resistance, leverage their expertise, address concerns transparently, and involve them in the design', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.3: Influential, expert and resistant stakeholders are the most critical to engage well.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.3 Stakeholders | Advanced', "question": 'In a complex banking transformation, a BA spends the first week solely identifying and analyzing stakeholders. This BEST reflects that:', "options": ["A) Stakeholder analysis is the PM's job", 'B) Only the sponsor needs to be identified at the start', 'C) BA tasks begin by documenting requirements', 'D) Early, exhaustive identification is fundamental: omitting stakeholders leads to missed needs and failed solutions'], "answer": 'D) Early, exhaustive identification is fundamental: omitting stakeholders leads to missed needs and failed solutions', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.3: Rigorous, early identification is a critical investment; the completeness of the requirements depends on it.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.3 Stakeholders | Expert', "question": "Mid-project, a BA discovers that a new regulatory body has jurisdiction over the solution's outputs. The MOST appropriate response is:", "options": ["A) Immediately update the register, analyze the new body's requirements and influence, assess the impact on existing requirements, and present findings to the sponsor", 'B) Continue; it was not in the original scope', 'C) Wait until go-live', 'D) Let the legal team handle it'], "answer": "A) Immediately update the register, analyze the new body's requirements and influence, assess the impact on existing requirements, and present findings to the sponsor", "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.3: Stakeholder identification is not one-off; new ones discovered mid-project must be analyzed and their requirements captured early.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.4 Requirements vs Designs | Foundational', "question": "'The system must let users filter results by date.' This is BEST classified as:", "options": ['A) Non-functional requirement', 'B) Business requirement', 'C) Functional solution requirement', 'D) Design artifact'], "answer": 'C) Functional solution requirement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.4: Filtering is a concrete system capability — a Functional solution requirement.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.4 Requirements vs Designs | Foundational', "question": 'A high-fidelity mockup with exact colors, typography and positions BEST represents:', "options": ['A) A design artifact', 'B) A business requirement', 'C) A stakeholder requirement', 'D) A non-functional requirement'], "answer": 'A) A design artifact', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.4: A detailed mockup describes how the solution will look — it is a design artifact.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.4 Requirements vs Designs | Intermediate', "question": "'The checkout screen must show a progress indicator for steps 1 to 4.' This is BEST classified as:", "options": ['A) Non-functional requirement', 'B) Business requirement', 'C) Functional requirement', 'D) Design artifact: it specifies UI implementation details rather than a business behavior'], "answer": 'D) Design artifact: it specifies UI implementation details rather than a business behavior', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.4: Specifying concrete visual UI elements crosses into design territory.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.4 Requirements vs Designs | Intermediate', "question": 'A BA keeps the requirements and design documents separate even though they produce both. The BEST reason per BABOK® v3 is:', "options": ['A) So that developers know what to read', 'B) Separating them preserves flexibility: requirements define the need; designs are one of many ways to meet it', 'C) The organization requires it', 'D) Requirements are more important'], "answer": 'B) Separating them preserves flexibility: requirements define the need; designs are one of many ways to meet it', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.4: The distinction preserves the flexibility of the solution space.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.4 Requirements vs Designs | Advanced', "question": 'A product owner writes acceptance criteria prescribing exact field names and pixel layout. Per BABOK® v3 these represent:', "options": ['A) Transition requirements', 'B) Design constraints embedded in the acceptance criteria', 'C) Non-functional requirements', 'D) Pure stakeholder requirements'], "answer": 'B) Design constraints embedded in the acceptance criteria', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.4: When the criteria prescribe implementation details, they cross into design.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.4 Requirements vs Designs | Expert', "question": 'A BA asks when an artifact shifts from requirement to design. The MOST precise answer is:', "options": ['A) Designs are created by the architect and requirements by the BA', 'B) The boundary is defined by abstraction and intent: solution-agnostic requirements (what/why) vs designs that describe how a specific solution will be structured; an artifact can cross it', 'C) When the sponsor approves it', 'D) When it passes from the BA to the developer'], "answer": 'B) The boundary is defined by abstraction and intent: solution-agnostic requirements (what/why) vs designs that describe how a specific solution will be structured; an artifact can cross it', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.4: The distinction is not by role or document, but by abstraction and intent.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.5 Integration | Foundational', "question": 'Using the Chapter 2 concepts, what must come BEFORE designing the solution architecture?', "options": ['A) Understand the Need, the Context and the Stakeholders, and define requirements', 'B) Write test cases', 'C) Select the vendor', 'D) Sign the contract'], "answer": 'A) Understand the Need, the Context and the Stakeholders, and define requirements', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.1, §2.3, §2.4: Understanding the problem and the requirements precedes the design of the solution.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.5 Integration | Intermediate', "question": "A BA asks: 'Are these requirements internally complete, consistent and unambiguous?' This BEST describes:", "options": ['A) Validation', 'B) Verification', 'C) Prioritization', 'D) Baseline'], "answer": 'B) Verification', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: Verification checks the internal quality of the requirements.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.5 Integration | Intermediate', "question": "A vague need ('make it secure') is refined into 'comply with ISO 27001 and encrypt sensitive data'. What happened?", "options": ['A) A functional requirement became a transition requirement', 'B) A vague stakeholder need was refined into measurable non-functional requirements', 'C) A business requirement became a stakeholder requirement', 'D) A design became a requirement'], "answer": 'B) A vague stakeholder need was refined into measurable non-functional requirements', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: Clarifying a vague quality expectation into measurable criteria produces non-functional requirements.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.5 Integration | Advanced', "question": 'A BA maps: compliance need → solution requirement → system design. This chain BEST illustrates:', "options": ['A) Traceability — the RLCM mechanism that links every solution element to a justified need', 'B) The difference between BA and PM', 'C) The BACCM in action', 'D) The sequentiality of the knowledge areas'], "answer": 'A) Traceability — the RLCM mechanism that links every solution element to a justified need', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: Traceability connects needs → requirements → designs, providing justification and impact analysis.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.5 Integration | Expert', "question": 'Benefits at 35% of target. The BACCM shows a correct Need, monitored Context, engaged Stakeholders and a Solution delivered as designed. The MOST likely remaining explanation is:', "options": ['A) The Solution, well built, does not deliver the expected Value in the real operating Context; the Value model assumed conditions that did not materialize', 'B) Documentation was missing', 'C) The PM failed to manage scope', 'D) Development did not follow specifications'], "answer": 'A) The Solution, well built, does not deliver the expected Value in the real operating Context; the Value model assumed conditions that did not materialize', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.1: Value realization depends on real conditions; a correct Solution can underperform if the Value model assumed factors that did not materialize.'},
    {"chapter": 'Ch2 §2.2 Requirements | Foundational', "question": "'The system must allow registering new customers with email validation.' This is BEST classified as:", "options": ['A) Business requirement', 'B) Stakeholder requirement', 'C) Functional solution requirement', 'D) Non-functional solution requirement'], "answer": 'C) Functional solution requirement', "explanation": 'BABOK® v3 §2.2: Registration with validation is a concrete capability — a Functional solution requirement.'},
